{"author":{"id":"f4f7e123ea38957901744390924e1710825e6d55b5113472173231f66fb2d926"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1781353410,"nonce":"tmi+Z+LChABoL5XZA1pZ7pn/Bak=","message":"Draft (2026-06-13, for review before posting):\n\nShow HN title: Show HN: GW-BASIC 2026 -- a portable C reimplementation with an AOT compiler\n\nBody:\nI've been rebuilding Microsoft GW-BASIC from scratch in portable C, cross-checked against the 8088 assembly Microsoft open-sourced in 2020, with the goal of being bug-compatible with the original rather than just close enough.\n\nIt's more than an interpreter. There's an ahead-of-time compiler that lowers tokenized .bas to C and builds a native binary against a small runtime. It links both ways with C and Fortran now: you can call compiled BASIC from a C/Fortran program, and call C functions from BASIC through a '$EXTERN pragma.\n\nIt runs on Linux/macOS/BSD via CMake, and on bare DOS/FreeDOS -- both 16-bit real mode (a 128 KB standalone .exe, no DOS extender) and 32-bit DOS/4GW. The full-screen editor renders through BIOS INT 10h, so it works on bare FreeDOS with no ANSI.SYS.\n\nOther bits: 100% token coverage (all 144 GW-BASIC tokens), MBF on-disk file compatibility, a hardware-I/O simulator (8253 PIT speaker, CGA registers), and a Jupyter kernel with inline Sixel graphics. MIT licensed.\n\nhttps://github.com/evvaletov/gw-basic-2026\n\nLobsters: same URL, tags retrocomputing + c + compilers, short authored-by comment on the AOT-compiler + DOS angle.","files":null}]}